(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cutaneous-bacterial-infections-quiz"]={"title":"Cutaneous Bacterial Infections Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the expected course of cellulitis on antibiotic therapy?","choices":["It improves visibly within hours, fever resolves within six hours, and erythema clears within three days","It worsens for the first week before any improvement, with fever expected to persist throughout","It resolves without any change in appearance, since the erythema is not caused by the infection","It may look worse on day one, fever usually resolves within twenty-four hours, inflammation over one to two weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fever beyond forty-eight hours prompts a change guided by culture. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"Which finding in a woman with persistent acne points toward polycystic ovarian syndrome?","choices":["Pustules pierced by a central hair over the thighs and trunk","Premenstrual flares of acne with an otherwise normal cycle","Hirsutism together with irregular or absent menses","Flushing with hot drinks and visible facial telangiectasias"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That combination indicates androgen hypersecretion. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A patient's acne has papules and pustules that have not settled on a topical retinoid with benzoyl peroxide. What is added next?","choices":["Oral isotretinoin straight away","A stronger topical retinoid","An oral antibiotic","A six-week oral corticosteroid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — an oral antibiotic joins the topical retinoid and benzoyl peroxide at this rung. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 15:40"},{"q":"Which class is the typical first-line topical therapy for comedonal and inflammatory acne?","choices":["Topical antimicrobials, which act against Cutibacterium acnes in the follicle","Topical dapsone gel, which is anti-inflammatory by an unknown mechanism","Topical azelaic acid, which is antimicrobial, comedolytic and anti-inflammatory","Topical retinoids, which are comedolytic and normalise hyperkeratinisation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — adapalene, tazarotene and tretinoin, generally the most effective for mild disease. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How does acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis present after impetigo?","choices":["Migratory arthritis, a new heart murmur and subcutaneous nodules over the elbows","Fever with a sandpaper rash, strawberry tongue and circumoral pallor","Painless jaundice with pale stools and progressive weight loss","Sudden oedema, haematuria with smoky or tea-coloured urine, proteinuria and hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — especially in three to seven year olds. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"What is the typical timing and distribution of hot tub folliculitis?","choices":["Onset three to seven days after a sore throat, on the face and extremities","Onset eight hours to five days after exposure, on the trunk, extremities and buttocks","Onset two to five days after trauma, around the fingernail folds of the hand","Onset within forty-eight hours, on the lower extremity with a demarcated border"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It usually spares the face, neck, soles and palms. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Why is Bactroban written as the ointment rather than the cream?","choices":["The cream causes more contact irritation on inflamed skin","The cream is not licensed for use on the face","The cream costs far more and is never covered","The cream is less effective against Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — roughly a hundred times the price, and the ointment is covered. A prescribing detail that appears on no slide. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 22:12"},{"q":"What causes hot tub folliculitis and why does it occur?","choices":["Streptococcus pyogenes, a Gram-positive organism, from a break in the skin","Pseudomonas aeruginosa, a Gram-negative organism, from inadequately chlorinated water","Staphylococcus aureus, a Gram-positive organism, from shared towels and razors","Corynebacterium minutissimum, a Gram-positive organism, from heat and humidity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Whirlpools, hot tubs, water slides and physiotherapy pools are the named sources. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Describe the classic appearance of non-bullous impetigo.","choices":["A vesicle over inflamed skin deepens into a dermal ulcer with a thick grey-yellow crust","Papules and pustules on an erythematous base, each pierced by a single central hair","A macule evolves into a vesicle or pustule that ruptures, leaving a honey-coloured adherent crust","Tense clear or cloudy bullae appear on intact skin and leave a collarette after they rupture"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, typically on the face and extremities, with regional lymphadenopathy common. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"Which portals of entry are named for cellulitis?","choices":["Tinea pedis, open lesion, trauma, surgical wound, insect bite, fissure and radiation therapy","Nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus, shaving, occlusive clothing and hot humid weather","Manicure, ingrown nail, hangnail and nail biting affecting the soft tissue of the finger","Contaminated whirlpools, hot tubs, water slides and inadequately chlorinated pools"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Those are distinct from the risk factors, which include diabetes and lymphoedema. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"When can cellulitis be managed without any diagnostic workup?","choices":["Limited area of involvement, minimal pain, no systemic signs and no risk factor for serious illness","Any case in which the patient is afebrile, regardless of the extent of the affected area","Any case occurring in a patient under fifty years of age with no history of diabetes","Only after blood cultures have returned negative and inflammatory markers are normal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Serious infections warrant blood cultures, punch biopsy, blood count and creatine phosphokinase. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"What causes chronic paronychia and who is at risk?","choices":["A bacterial infection of the soft tissue around the nail, with Staphylococcus aureus commonest","A dermatophyte infection of the nail plate itself, with Trichophyton rubrum commonest","A foreign body reaction to a cut hair entering the skin, commonest in those who shave","An inflammatory reaction of the proximal nail fold to irritants and allergens, with Candida albicans commonest"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — diabetics, laundry workers, cleaners, cooks, bartenders, dishwashers and swimmers. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 120"},{"q":"How is chronic paronychia treated?","choices":["Topical mupirocin applied to the nail fold three times daily until the swelling settles","Keep the hands dry and use a broad-spectrum topical antifungal, with oral fluconazole in severe cases","Warm soaks three times daily with incision and drainage if purulent material has collected","Oral cephalexin for ten days with removal of the nail plate if there is no early response"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — treat the underlying inflammation and infection together. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A patient with folliculitis has not responded to a topical antibiotic and the infection is now extensive. Which oral agents apply?","choices":["Doxycycline or minocycline","Cephalexin or dicloxacillin","Penicillin V or amoxicillin","Erythromycin or clarithromycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, ciprofloxacin or linezolid if resistance is suspected. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which laboratory abnormalities are common in erysipelas, and what do they contribute?","choices":["Thrombocytopenia with mild hyponatraemia and transaminitis — and diagnostic in almost every case","Eosinophilia with a raised immunoglobulin E — diagnostic when combined with the rash","Entirely normal inflammatory markers — which is what confirms the diagnosis","Leukocytosis with raised erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein — common but not diagnostic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis remains clinical in a classic presentation. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"What defines folliculitis?","choices":["Inflammatory cells within the wall and ostia of the hair follicle, forming a follicular pustule","A deep-seated abscess of the hair follicle and the adjacent subcutaneous tissue","Inflammation of the cutaneous apocrine glands of the axilla, groin or perineum","A foreign body reaction to a cut hair that curves back into the wall of the follicle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The causes include infection, physical injury and chemical irritation. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which laboratory and imaging studies are appropriate in classic erysipelas?","choices":["None routinely — the diagnosis is clinical, and blood and tissue cultures have very low yield","Blood and tissue cultures in every case, since organism identification guides the antibiotic","Computed tomography of the affected limb to define the depth of tissue involvement","Punch biopsy of the leading edge to distinguish erysipelas from contact dermatitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Leukocytosis and raised inflammatory markers are common but not diagnostic. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Where should a patient with necrotizing fasciitis be admitted?","choices":["A surgical intensive care unit, ideally a burn or trauma centre","A general medical ward with hourly observations","A dermatology inpatient unit for wound management","An ambulatory unit for daily dressing changes and review"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a team approach with consultations is required. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 131"},{"q":"What isolation advice is given for a child with impetigo?","choices":["Isolate until treatment has been underway for twenty-four to forty-eight hours","Isolate until every crusted lesion has completely healed and the skin is clear","No isolation is needed at any point, since impetigo does not spread person to person","Isolate for a full ten days from the appearance of the very first crusted lesion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and do not share towels, clothing, bath water, washcloths or razors. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which layers does cellulitis involve, and which organisms cause it?","choices":["The superficial epidermis alone, from Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pyogenes","The stratum corneum of intertriginous skin, from Corynebacterium minutissimum","The deeper dermis and subcutaneous tissue, from Group A streptococci or Staphylococcus aureus","The upper dermis and superficial lymphatics, almost always from Group A streptococci"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It may be purulent or non-purulent, which changes the antibiotic choice. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"Where does erythrasma most commonly appear?","choices":["Lower extremity in most patients, almost never affecting both legs","Inner thighs, crural region, scrotum, and between the fourth and fifth toes","Back of the neck, face, axillae and buttocks, favouring areas of friction","Face and extremities, spreading by scratching and self-inoculation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Less common sites are the axilla, under the breasts and intergluteal folds. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"In whom does pseudofolliculitis barbae most commonly occur?","choices":["Adolescents of any background during puberty","Black and brown males, or anyone with curlier facial or body hair","Fair-skinned males who shave daily with a multi-blade razor","Females who wax rather than shave"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — hair curvature is the mechanism, not hygiene, and she called it very common. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 26:14"},{"q":"How does purulence change the antibiotic choice in cellulitis?","choices":["Purulent cellulitis prompts cover for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus","Purulent cellulitis prompts a switch to penicillin V to cover Group A streptococcus","Purulent cellulitis prompts topical mupirocin in addition to the oral antibiotic","Purulent cellulitis prompts oral fluconazole because yeast is likely to be present"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, doxycycline, clindamycin or linezolid. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"When should cultures be obtained in impetigo?","choices":["Only when the lesions have failed to respond after a full two-week course of topical mupirocin","Only in children under three years of age, since they are the group at highest risk of complications","When the patient is at high risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, or when post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is present","In every case at first presentation, since staphylococcal and streptococcal disease are treated differently from one another"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a health-care worker or teacher is the example given of high risk. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Which feature distinguishes contact dermatitis from cellulitis?","choices":["Warmth and tenderness with a rapidly expanding border","A raised plaque with a sharply demarcated edge","Pain out of proportion to the physical examination","Itching and vesicles with an absence of fever"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — cellulitis is tender and warm rather than itchy. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"What is hidradenitis suppurativa and where does it occur?","choices":["Inflammation of the eccrine sweat glands, most often on the palms, soles and the axillary vault","Infection of the upper third of the stratum corneum in intertriginous skin under heat and humidity","Infection of the hair follicle extending into the adjacent subcutaneous fat of the neck and back","Inflammation of cutaneous apocrine glands, most often in the axilla, and also groin, breasts or perineum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is also called acne inversa because it resembles acne vulgaris. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"How does a carbuncle differ from a furuncle at presentation?","choices":["It presents as a plaque raised above the skin with a demarcated edge","It is extremely painful and more often produces malaise, chills and fever","It is painless and produces no systemic symptoms of any kind at all","It presents as a single opening with a surrounding zone of erythema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Systemic symptoms are more common with a carbuncle. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which trait is shared by the oral agents used for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus skin infection?","choices":["Penicillin V, erythromycin and clarithromycin recur as the core options","Mupirocin, retapamulin and fusidic acid recur as the core options","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, clindamycin and doxycycline recur as the core options","Dicloxacillin, cephalexin and amoxicillin-clavulanate recur as the core options"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with linezolid and ciprofloxacin added in particular settings. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"How is acute paronychia treated?","choices":["Warm soaks for twenty minutes three times daily in mild cases; incision and drainage in severe cases","A broad-spectrum topical antifungal in all cases, with oral fluconazole if the disease is severe","Immediate nail plate removal in all cases, since the nail harbours the responsible organism","Oral antibiotics alone in all cases, since incision of the nail fold risks permanent deformity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with oral antibiotics and cultures to rule out resistant organisms where appropriate. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"What level of the skin does impetigo infect, and which organisms cause it?","choices":["An infection of the tissue surrounding muscle and fascia, usually polymicrobial in nature","A superficial epidermal infection, most often Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pyogenes","An infection of the upper dermis and superficial lymphatics, most often Streptococcus pyogenes","An infection of the deeper dermis and subcutaneous tissue, streptococcal or staphylococcal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is very contagious and autoinoculable, and common in infants and children. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 85"}]};
